--TEST--
A property write through a `mixed` receiver checks an array's elements, a shape's fields and every arm of a union against the declared type, so a typed read never sees an object of the wrong class — `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A `Kennel` declares what its properties hold. Each write below goes through
// a `mixed` variable, so the program checks the value when it runs.
// A value of the declared type is written. Any other value throws an error.
class Animal { public string $name = "tom"; }
class Dog extends Animal { public int $legs = 4; }

class Kennel {
    public array<Dog> $dogs = [];
    public {pet: Dog} $home;
    public int|Dog $either = 0;
    public ?int $count = null;
    public ?array<Dog> $spare = null;

    public function constructor() { $this->home = {pet: new Dog()}; }

    public static function write(mixed $m, string $field, mixed $value): string {
        try {
            if ($field == "dogs") { $m->dogs = $value; }
            if ($field == "home") { $m->home = $value; }
            if ($field == "either") { $m->either = $value; }
            if ($field == "count") { $m->count = $value; }
            if ($field == "spare") { $m->spare = $value; }
            return "written";
        } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }
}

var $k = new Kennel();
echo Kennel::write($k, "dogs", [new Dog(), new Dog()]), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "dogs", [new Dog(), new Animal()]), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "dogs", ["dog"]), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "home", {pet: new Dog()}), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "home", {pet: new Animal()}), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "home", {cat: new Dog()}), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "either", 3), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "either", new Dog()), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "either", new Animal()), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "either", "three"), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "count", null), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "count", 2.5), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "spare", [new Dog()]), "\n";
echo Kennel::write($k, "spare", [new Animal()]), "\n";

// The refused writes stored nothing, so every typed read finds a `Dog`.
Dog $d = $k->dogs[1];
echo $d->legs, " ", $k->home->pet->legs, " ", Core\Arr::count($k->dogs), "\n";
--EXPECT--
written
`Kennel` declares field `dogs` as `array<Dog>`, so an array holding an object of class `Animal` cannot be written to it
`Kennel` declares field `dogs` as `array<Dog>`, so an array holding a string cannot be written to it
written
`Kennel` declares field `home` as `{pet: Dog}`, so an object whose `pet` is an object of class `Animal` cannot be written to it
`Kennel` declares field `home` as `{pet: Dog}`, so an object with no field `pet` cannot be written to it
written
written
`Kennel` declares field `either` as `int|Dog`, so an object of class `Animal` cannot be written to it
`Kennel` declares field `either` as `int|Dog`, so a string cannot be written to it
written
`Kennel` declares field `count` as `?int`, so a float cannot be written to it
written
`Kennel` declares field `spare` as `?array<Dog>`, so an array holding an object of class `Animal` cannot be written to it
4 4 2
